A crystalline city floating in a tide-pool’s reverse gravity;  
A squid with typewriter tentacles whispering sonnets in Morse;  
A machine that turns sighs into edible clouds;  
A ritual where people swap nightmares for new shoes;  
A library built inside a black-hole’s event horizon;  
A song composed entirely of whale-songs in reverse;  
A feeling-object: “Grief in a porcelain hourglass that only laughs when you look away.”



It glows like a dying neon library. Its ink spools into sonnets, each syllable a falling star. I’d first stare at its eyes—goggles of liquid glass reflecting typewriter keys. It shuffles through coral shelves of inked pages, clacking keys underwater, each word a bubble that bursts into rain. Strange bioluminescent jellyfish dance in its wake, glowing blue with guilt. The water sings low, like a harp strummed by silence. Its arms crackle when it types joy. I’d press my palm to its back and feel the keys vibrate like beating hearts. It’d blink and write my name in steam. I’d steal a line. I’d be its ghost-ink.

